{"task_id": "HumanEval/8", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List, Tuple\n\ndef sum_product(numbers: List[int]) -> Tuple[int, int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the sum and product of a list of integers efficiently in a single pass.\n\n    This function is a pure, stateless computational component designed to be a\n    reusable piece of logic for mathematical operations.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: A list of integers for which to calculate the sum and product.\n                 The list can be empty.\n\n    Returns:\n        A tuple containing two integers: the sum of the elements and the\n        product of the elements. For an empty list, it returns (0, 1),\n        representing the additive and multiplicative identities, respectively.\n    \"\"\"\n    current_sum = 0\n    current_product = 1\n\n    for number in numbers:\n        current_sum += number\n        current_product *= number\n\n    return (current_sum, current_product)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/9", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef rolling_max(numbers: List[int]) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes a rolling maximum from a given sequence of integers.\n\n    For each element in the input sequence, this function determines the\n    maximum value encountered from the start of the sequence up to that element.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: A list of integers for which to calculate the rolling maximum.\n\n    Returns:\n        A new list of integers representing the rolling maximum. Returns an\n        empty list if the input list is empty.\n    \"\"\"\n    # 1. Handle Edge Case: Empty Input\n    if not numbers:\n        return []\n\n    # 2. Initialization\n    result_list = []\n    # Initialize the running maximum with the first element of the input list.\n    running_max = numbers[0]\n\n    # 3. Iteration\n    # Loop through each number in the input list.\n    for number in numbers:\n        # 3a. Compare and Update\n        # If the current number is greater than the running maximum,\n        # update the running maximum.\n        if number > running_max:\n            running_max = number\n\n        # 3b. Append to Result\n        # Append the current state of the running maximum to the result list.\n        result_list.append(running_max)\n\n    # 4. Return Result\n    return result_list\n```"}
