{"task_id": "HumanEval/40", "solution": "```python\ndef triples_sum_to_zero(l: list[int]) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if there are three distinct elements in the list that sum to zero.\n\n    Args:\n        l: A list of integers.\n\n    Returns:\n        True if a zero-sum triple exists, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(l)\n\n    # Edge Case Check: If the list has fewer than three elements, no such triple can exist.\n    if n < 3:\n        return False\n\n    # Sort the list. This is crucial for the two-pointer approach and takes O(n log n).\n    l.sort()\n\n    # Outer Loop: Iterate through the list, fixing the first element of the potential triple.\n    # We only need to go up to n-2 because we need at least two more elements (left and right).\n    for i in range(n - 2):\n        # Initialize two pointers for the subarray to the right of the fixed element.\n        left = i + 1\n        right = n - 1\n\n        # Inner Loop (Two-Pointer Search)\n        while left < right:\n            current_sum = l[i] + l[left] + l[right]\n\n            # Check for Solution: If the sum is zero, we found a valid triple.\n            if current_sum == 0:\n                return True\n            # Adjust Pointers:\n            # If the sum is less than zero, we need a larger sum. Move the left pointer\n            # to the right to include a larger number.\n            elif current_sum < 0:\n                left += 1\n            # If the sum is greater than zero, we need a smaller sum. Move the right pointer\n            # to the left to include a smaller number.\n            else:  # current_sum > 0\n                right -= 1\n\n    # No Solution Found: If the loops complete, no zero-sum triple was found.\n    return False\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/41", "solution": "```python\ndef car_race_collision(n: int):\n    \"\"\"\n    Imagine a road that's a perfectly straight infinitely long line.\n    n cars are driving left to right;  simultaneously, a different set of n cars\n    are driving right to left.   The two sets of cars start out being very far from\n    each other.  All cars move in the same speed.  Two cars are said to collide\n    when a car that's moving left to right hits a car that's moving right to left.\n    However, the cars are infinitely sturdy and strong; as a result, they continue moving\n    in their trajectory as if they did not collide.\n\n    This function outputs the number of such collisions.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Each of the n cars moving from left to right will eventually cross paths\n    # with each of the n cars moving from right to left. Since they are\n    # infinitely sturdy and pass through each other, every possible pairing\n    # of a left-bound car and a right-bound car will result in one collision.\n    # The total number of such pairs is n * n.\n    return n * n\n```"}
