{"task_id": "HumanEval/43", "solution": "```python\ndef pairs_sum_to_zero(l: list[int]) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if there are two distinct elements in a list of integers that sum to zero.\n\n    Args:\n        l: A list of integers.\n\n    Returns:\n        True if a zero-sum pair exists, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    # A list with fewer than 2 elements cannot contain a pair.\n    if len(l) < 2:\n        return False\n\n    # Initialize a hash set for efficient lookups.\n    seen_numbers = set()\n\n    # Iterate through each number in the input list.\n    for number in l:\n        # The complement for 'number' is '-number'.\n        # Check if the complement has been seen before.\n        if -number in seen_numbers:\n            # A pair is found.\n            return True\n        \n        # If no pair is found, add the current number to the set\n        # to be checked against subsequent elements.\n        seen_numbers.add(number)\n    \n    # If the loop completes, no pair was found.\n    return False\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/44", "solution": "```python\ndef change_base(x: int, base: int) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Converts a non-negative integer from its base-10 representation to a\n    string representation in a specified target base.\n\n    Args:\n        x: The non-negative integer to be converted (x >= 0).\n        base: The target numerical base (2 <= base <= 9).\n\n    Returns:\n        A string representing the number `x` in the specified `base`.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If `base` is outside the valid range [2, 9].\n        ValueError: If `x` is a negative integer.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Input Validation\n    if not 2 <= base <= 9:\n        raise ValueError(\"Base must be between 2 and 9.\")\n    if x < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input number cannot be negative.\")\n\n    # Edge Case Handling for Zero\n    if x == 0:\n        return \"0\"\n\n    # Conversion Loop\n    digits_list = []\n    current_number = x\n    while current_number > 0:\n        remainder = current_number % base\n        digits_list.append(remainder)\n        current_number = current_number // base\n\n    # Result Construction\n    # The digits are generated in reverse order, so we reverse the list\n    # and join the elements (converted to strings) to form the final string.\n    return \"\".join(map(str, reversed(digits_list)))\n\n```"}
