{"task_id": "HumanEval/46", "solution": "```python\ndef fib4(n: int):\n    \"\"\"The Fib4 number sequence is a sequence similar to the Fibbonacci sequnece that's defined as follows:\n    fib4(0) -> 0\n    fib4(1) -> 0\n    fib4(2) -> 2\n    fib4(3) -> 0\n    fib4(n) -> fib4(n-1) + fib4(n-2) + fib4(n-3) + fib4(n-4).\n    Please write a function to efficiently compute the n-th element of the fib4 number sequence.  Do not use recursion.\n    >>> fib4(5)\n    4\n    >>> fib4(6)\n    8\n    >>> fib4(7)\n    14\n    \"\"\"\n    if n < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input must be a non-negative integer.\")\n\n    if n <= 3:\n        base_cases = [0, 0, 2, 0]\n        return base_cases[n]\n\n    # Initialize the \"sliding window\" with the first four values of the sequence\n    # a = fib4(0), b = fib4(1), c = fib4(2), d = fib4(3)\n    a, b, c, d = 0, 0, 2, 0\n\n    # Loop from 4 up to n, calculating each subsequent term\n    for _ in range(4, n + 1):\n        # Calculate the next value as the sum of the previous four\n        next_val = a + b + c + d\n        # \"Slide\" the window one position forward\n        a, b, c, d = b, c, d, next_val\n\n    # After the loop, d holds the value for fib4(n)\n    return d\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/47", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List, Union\n\ndef median(l: List[Union[int, float]]) -> Union[int, float]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the statistical median of a list of numbers.\n\n    Args:\n        l: A list containing numeric, sortable elements (e.g., integers, floats).\n\n    Returns:\n        The calculated median of the elements in the list. The return type may\n        be a float even with integer inputs if the list has an even number of elements.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If the input list `l` is empty.\n        TypeError: If the list contains non-numeric or non-comparable types.\n    \"\"\"\n    # 1. Input Validation: Check if the list is empty.\n    if not l:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input list cannot be empty to calculate the median.\")\n\n    # 2. Sorting: Create a sorted copy of the input list.\n    sorted_l = sorted(l)\n\n    # 3. Size and Index Calculation: Determine the number of elements and middle index.\n    n = len(sorted_l)\n    mid_index = n // 2\n\n    # 4. Median Computation (Odd Length)\n    if n % 2 != 0:\n        return sorted_l[mid_index]\n    # 5. Median Computation (Even Length)\n    else:\n        # The median is the arithmetic mean of the two central elements.\n        return (sorted_l[mid_index - 1] + sorted_l[mid_index]) / 2\n```"}
